\begin_layout Part
Reed blocks coupling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take a reed plate off its block and hold it in the hand. It plays, but it plays alone. Put it back on the block, over its chamber, under the soundboard, behind its pallet, and it plays with everything around it. This part is about that everything: the chamber of wood below the tongue, the hole in the soundboard above it, the channel under the pallet, and the other tongue of the same note that breathes the same air. The maker chooses all of it with a saw, a chisel and a pot of wax. The physics says what each choice does to the start of the note, to its pitch and to its sound.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The method of this part is the one of chapter 2. A finite element model (Elmer) gives the passive answer of the air in the chamber, seen from the slot of the tongue. A small network of masses of air, springs of air and orifices (the semi-analytical model coupled_reeds) receives these numbers and makes the tongue oscillate. Every number quoted below is either a finite element result on the chambers of the R12 block, a prediction of that small network, or a figure from the workshop. The status of each one is said in the text, because the small network is known to be wrong on several points (chapter 2 and the audit of the two-reed model, docs/audit_deux_anches.md): its tongues travel too far and its flows are too large, and the experiment of the sandwich reed refutes its mechanism of start-up. Its tendencies are kept; its pascals are not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Reed block design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A reed block is a wooden comb. Each tooth of the comb is a wall, and between two walls lies a chamber. The plate with its two tongues closes the chamber on one side; the soundboard, with a hole, closes it on the other. In the R12 block studied here, each chamber is made of two cells, called A and B, separated by a central partition that changes side in the middle of the block. The air of such a chamber was meshed with second-order tetrahedra and its modes computed with Elmer 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "elmer_models"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, the mesh being made with Gmsh 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "geuzaine2009gmsh"
literal "true"

\end_inset
. The walls and the plates are rigid in the model, the slots are closed (tongues at rest), the air is at 20 degrees Celsius. The far end of the hole opens on a large volume at zero pressure, the pallet being far away.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What came out first is a number for each chamber. With a hole of 8 by 25 mm through a soundboard 8 mm thick (dimensions taken from the small network, not measured on the instrument), the first air resonance of the twelve chambers of the R12 goes from 1142 Hz (chamber 1, the largest, 18.3 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

 of cells) to 1575 Hz (chamber 12, 10.2 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

). A second mode, where the air of cell A swings against the air of cell B, lies between 1402 and 2017 Hz. A mesh of 2 mm is enough for a plan of experiments: the first mode of chamber 12 moves from 1575.5 Hz to 1574.1 Hz when the mesh is refined from 2 mm to 0.8 mm, which is 0.1 per cent or 1.5 cent. The solver was checked on a rigid box of 50 by 15 by 10 mm, where it gives 3430.0 Hz for the exact 
\begin_inset Formula $c/2L=3430$
\end_inset

 Hz, and on the same box open at one end (1715.0, 5145.0 and 8575.0 Hz for the quarter-wave series).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Glues or solid RB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A block can be built up from pieces, partitions glued on a base, or cut from one solid piece of wood. The maker feels the difference in the hand and hears it, or believes he hears it. What does the physics of this work say about it? Honestly, little so far, and it is better to say what it does not say.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All the acoustic computations of this work take the walls as rigid. A rigid wall does two things: it keeps the air in, and it gives the air nothing back. A real wall of a glued block does the same two things imperfectly. A glue joint that is not tight is a leak, and a thin wall that flexes adds a little compliance to the chamber and takes a little energy from it at each cycle. The finite element model is also without losses: its resonance peaks are infinitely sharp, and the real ones, as the repository says, will be rounder. The resistance of a real chamber is calibrated from a measurement, not computed (section on the chamber seen from the slot, gesture (b) of the experimental protocol).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The workshop criterion for the tightness of a block is a figure from the training booklet of Ewen, a proposed criterion, not yet measured on his instruments: a glued block, bare or furnished with its plates, should leak less than 0.05 litre per minute under 500 Pa. The way to measure it is the flow meter of the tuning table, or the pressure decay test of the bench (command LEAKTEST of the firmware, analysed by the function fit_decay of banc_recherche/leak.py, which fits 
\begin_inset Formula $p(t)=p_{0}e^{-t/\tau}$
\end_inset

 on the logarithm of the pressure and returns the time constant and a conductance).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): is there a measurable difference between a glued chamber and a solid one? Two measurements, both cheap: the leak at 500 Pa of each block on the tuning table (flow meter SFM3000, or the gasometer and the U-tube of the workshop), and the quality factor of one chamber excited by a short sweep from a small loudspeaker and recorded by the microphone (ring-down, banc_recherche/ringdown.py). A chamber whose walls flex should show a lower Q. Which block, which chamber, and on what day?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Fluid organization in cavity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Follow the air. On the push, it leaves the bellows, passes through the slot of the tongue, fills the chamber, climbs through the hole of the soundboard into the channel under the pallet, lifts the pallet and goes out. On the draw, the same road is run in the other direction: the outside air enters by the pallet, goes down the hole, fills the chamber, passes the other tongue of the plate and ends in the bellows, which is in depression. In both cases the tongue stands between two pressures, and it is their difference that pushes it out of its slot and drives the air through.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each element of that road can be measured with a calliper, and each one becomes a simple thing in the model. The chamber is a spring of air: a volume 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 gives a compliance 
\begin_inset Formula $V/(\gamma P_{\mathrm{atm}})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is the adiabatic constant of air (about 1.4) and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{\mathrm{atm}}$
\end_inset

 the atmospheric pressure. The hole of the soundboard and the opening of the pallet are masses of air, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\ell/S$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 is the density of air, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

 the effective length of the passage (its real length plus the end corrections, about 0.8 times the equivalent radius on each side) and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 its area; they also carry an orifice loss, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho q|q|/(2(C_{d}S)^{2})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is the flow and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{d}$
\end_inset

 a discharge coefficient. The channel under the pallet is a volume common to the two tongues of the note. This is the whole network of coupled_reeds, and everything in it is derived from the conservation of mass and the law of Bernoulli for a short passage of incompressible air. What is not derived is the dimensions: chamber 35 by 15 by 15 mm, hole 8 by 25 by 20 mm, channel 4 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

, pallet 3 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

 by 10 mm are guessed orders of magnitude of a medium reed block, and each is to be taken on the instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Such a network of lumped elements is valid as long as every dimension is small compared with the wavelength: at 440 Hz the wavelength is about 78 cm and a chamber is 3 cm long. The resonances of the network lie far above the fundamentals of the tongues: chamber and hole near 2 kHz with the guessed dimensions (1142 to 1575 Hz with the R12 chambers computed by Elmer), channel and pallet near 5 kHz. They colour the high partials; they do not make the note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Section aperture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the bench of this work, the air does not come from a hand but from a motor, and the passage between the air supply and the tongue is a slot opened by a screw. Its width is 15 mm; the screw sets its length; the area is the product. In the plan of experiments inherited from the 2020 bench (Mesures.py), this area is the factor called Section, with 8 steps, crossed with 8 steps of pallet angle and with the steps of pressure, each point measured in both directions of pressure. This is the aperture of this section: how much the supply is throttled before the tongue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Why does it matter? Because the bellows is not an ideal source. An ideal pressure source holds its pressure whatever the flow; an ideal flow source holds its flow whatever the pressure. A real bellows, like the turbine of the bench, gives less flow when the pressure rises. In the model this is written 
\begin_inset Formula $q=q_{0}-p/R$
\end_inset

: the flow 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 that really arrives is the set flow 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{0}$
\end_inset

 minus the pressure 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 divided by an internal resistance 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

. The slope 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is read directly on the bench: at a fixed motor command, sweep the Section factor, note the pairs of pressure and flow, and plot pressure against flow. A vertical line is a flow source, a horizontal line a pressure source, an oblique line a real one, and its slope is 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

. The repository calls this experiment E1 and puts it first, because every amplitude the model predicts depends on it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The value of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 in the model has its own history, and it is worth telling. It was first set to 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times10^{8}$
\end_inset

 Pa s m
\begin_inset Formula $^{-3}$
\end_inset

 without any measurement. At that stiffness the tongue of a 55 mm blade was allowed to travel 110 mm at full loudness, which is absurd. Sweeping 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 showed that 
\begin_inset Formula $5\times10^{6}$
\end_inset

 Pa s m
\begin_inset Formula $^{-3}$
\end_inset

 gives travels of about 2 mm all over the keyboard, and the smallest pitch deviations; it corresponds to a supply restriction of about 8 mm
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

, the order of magnitude of a reed block channel. At 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times10^{6}$
\end_inset

 nothing starts any more. The number 
\begin_inset Formula $5\times10^{6}$
\end_inset

 is therefore a choice made so that the model stays in the range of a real accordion, not a measurement; the measurement is E1. Note also that the choking pressure, above which the tongue is blown out of its slot and stops, does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 at all in the one-reed model (379.8 Pa for the reference tongue, whatever the source): it is set by the geometry of the slot, not by the supply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a second aperture, the hole of the soundboard. The numerical plan of experiments on the chambers (research/fem/sommier/seuils_cavite.py) sweeps its area from 25 to 400 mm
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

, and a floor of 100 mm
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

 is kept in the proposal for the R12, so as not to throttle the air that the tongue consumes. That floor is a hypothesis, half of the assumed present hole, to be confirmed by the flow measured on the bench.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the characteristic of your source, pressure against flow, at a fixed motor command and for the eight Section steps (experiment E1), with the flow meter SFM3000 and the pressure sensor of the bench, or a water U-tube. One slope, one number. Which day?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pressure and Flow : impedance 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Impedance is a word for a simple question: when I push a flow, what pressure do I get back? The ratio of the two is the impedance. The bench of this work measures it in the crudest and most useful way, from the pressure sensor and the flow meter during a held note: banc_recherche/impedance.py takes the mean of pressure over flow, and also the product of the two, which is the pneumatic power that the player spends. This is the impedance of the whole instrument seen from the bellows, at zero frequency, and it is what the hand feels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The acoustic impedance seen by the tongue is another thing, and chapter 2 computed it. When the tongue pushes a small oscillating flow 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 through its slot, the chamber, the hole and the outside give back a pressure 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

; the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $Z(f)=p/q$
\end_inset

, frequency by frequency, is all that the surroundings do to the tongue. Elmer solves the Helmholtz equation for it and the result is condensed in the Foster form, a mass of air in series with a sum of lossless resonators. The main result is a sign. Far below the chamber resonance, which is where every tongue plays, the open chamber behaves as a mass of air: the air pushed by the tongue escapes through the hole and the tongue feels its inertia. A closed cavity behaves as a spring. In the band of the tongue the closed cavity of the old time-domain model was wrong by 12 400 per cent for the bass reed, 494 per cent for the medium and 232 per cent for the high one; one Foster resonator brings the error under 9 per cent and two under 0.5 per cent. The chamber of a reed block is not a box: it is a mass of air hanging on a hole.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Measuring an acoustic impedance with almost nothing is possible, and the software for it is already in the repository. The two-microphone method of the standard ISO 10534-2 puts two small microphones in a tube in front of the sample (a chamber, a pallet, a mute) and a loudspeaker at the other end; from the ratio of the two pressures and the spacing of the microphones one obtains the reflection coefficient, the normalised impedance and the absorption of the sample (banc_recherche/transfer.py, functions estimate_H12 and reflection_impedance). The sound card of the bench offers three channels, one stereo input and one mono, which is enough. The rig itself, a tube and two cheap measurement microphones, has not been built.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the first two-microphone measurement on a chamber of the R12, with the block closed by its plate and the hole open, to compare with the Elmer curve of the same chamber (first pole predicted at 1140 Hz for chamber 1). A plastic tube, two microphones, the sweep of frf.py. Who lends the second microphone, and when?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Acoustic radiation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where does the sound come out? In the model of this work, through the pallet. A small opening breathing in and out, small compared with the wavelength, radiates like a monopole: at a distance 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 the pressure is 
\begin_inset Formula $p(r,t)=\rho/(4\pi r)\cdot\mathrm{d}q_{p}/\mathrm{d}t$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{p}$
\end_inset

 is the flow through the pallet. This is derived, and valid as long as 
\begin_inset Formula $ka$
\end_inset

, the product of the wavenumber by the radius of the opening, stays small: about 0.08 at 440 Hz for the guessed pallet, and up to 0.9 near 5 kHz, where the formula ceases to hold. The radiated power is computed on the spectrum of the pallet flow with the radiation resistance of a baffled opening, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\omega^{2}/(2\pi c)$
\end_inset

, and checked numerically: for a known sinusoidal flow the function returns 
\begin_inset Formula $2.1277\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

 W where the formula 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\omega^{2}Q^{2}/(4\pi c)$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $2.1279\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

 W.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One lesson of chapter 2 is repeated here because it is easy to forget. A free reed radiates by its modulated flow, not by the pressure of its chamber. The spectrum to compare with a microphone is that of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{d}q/\mathrm{d}t$
\end_inset

. Counted that way, the one-reed model has twelve harmonics above minus 30 dB; counted on the chamber pressure, and with the fundamental taken at the loudest partial, it seemed to have two. The model was not poor; it was read wrongly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What the model says about efficiency, the share of the breath that becomes sound, is not to be trusted yet. One version gave 
\begin_inset Formula $1.5\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

 for one tongue and 
\begin_inset Formula $2.9\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

 for two tongues in phase; a later version, with the mechanism of the closing section, gave 1 to 3 per cent, which the audit calls suspect. Both numbers follow from tongue travels and flows that the audit already knows to be too large. And the hypothesis that the pallet is the only source is incomplete: the bellows, the box and the plate radiate too, and nothing in this work has measured how much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the acoustic power of one note against the pneumatic power spent, with a calibrated microphone (the calibration of the microphone has not been done) and the pressure and flow of the bench. One ratio, for one reed, at 1.5 times the threshold. This single number would judge the efficiency of every model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Impedance and inverse problem solving
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The direct problem is to compute what a known geometry does. The inverse problem is the maker's problem: from what is heard or measured, to find the geometry, or the few numbers that stand for it. This work meets it three times, and each time the same rule is applied: calibrate the least certain parameters on the most robust measurements, and keep the remaining observables as predictions. A model fitted on everything can no longer be contradicted, and then it teaches nothing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, from the finite elements. Sweeping 70 frequencies through Elmer for one chamber took 487 s. Instead, the poles of the impedance are taken from one eigenvalue computation, the residues from 14 harmonic solutions far from the poles, and the Foster form is rebuilt; on the same mesh it reproduces the sweep to 0.017 per cent (median) in 53 s. The first resonator then hands the network its effective volume, 
\begin_inset Formula $V=\rho c^{2}/a_{1}$
\end_inset

, and the effective length of the hole, 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell=(a_{1}/\omega_{1}^{2})S/\rho$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{1}$
\end_inset

 are the residue and the angular frequency of that resonator and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 the area of the hole. For chamber 1 under the bass tongue: 17.79 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

 and 25.8 mm, against 18.26 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

 of geometric cells and 8 mm of real thickness. The effective length is three times the real one: the end corrections are the least certain dimension of the whole block.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second, from a plucked tongue on its chamber (gesture (b) of chapter 2). The shift of the plucked frequency, predicted at 1.2 to 3.5 cents downward, calibrates the share 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa^{2}$
\end_inset

 of the swept volume that really goes through the chamber; the measured frequency of the air resonator calibrates the effective length of the hole, the volume being kept from the drawing; and the damping of that resonator gives the loss resistance that the lossless finite element model does not have, through 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta_{H}=(R/2)\sqrt{C/L_{t}}$
\end_inset

 for a series resistance, compliance and inertance. The measurement is cheap, a microphone and a toothpick, and it has a verdict built in: a shift of the wrong sign, or three times too large, or a resonator more than 10 per cent away from Elmer, would mean that the coupling through the slot must be reformulated, not re-tuned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Third, from a played sound. Here the honest answer is that a single note is not enough. Fitting a source and a resonator on the envelope of one note is under-determined: the envelope passes through the tops of the partials and mixes the two. The repository measured how many notes are needed to separate them, about ten spread over the compass, because below six the resonance is poorly located and above twelve the gain is marginal. The identification tool keeps two columns, what is measured and what is assumed, and prints both, because a parameter assumed and taken for measured is the surest way to be wrong for a long time. Before any optimisation in ten dimensions, a Plackett-Burman plan of experiments on the parameters of the model says which ones matter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A cautionary tale belongs here, about the frequency shift as a measure of the chamber. An early version of the one-reed model predicted that the played note stands 16 per cent above the plucked one, with an effective volume of 40 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

, and proposed to read the volume from that shift. The shift was real in the model but came from a term of swept volume that a free reed does not have: it compressed its chamber like a piston, which a tongue sitting in its slot does not do. Once that term was removed, the geometric chamber of 7.9 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

 was enough for the model to start, and the shift fell to tens of cents in the bass and a few cents above (plus 30 cents at 102 Hz, plus 2 cents at 440 Hz in that closed-cavity version), then changed sign once the hole of the soundboard entered the network. The inverse problem is only as good as the direct model it inverts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Reed block geometry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How deep should a chamber be, how wide its hole? The maker has rules of thumb, often inherited. Here is what a numerical plan of experiments on the small network says, with its limits stated first: one mode of blade, uniform steel blades, guessed lift and clearance, a mechanism of start-up that the sandwich experiment refutes. What is read is how the threshold moves when the chamber is changed, not the value of the threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The chamber and its hole form a Helmholtz resonator of frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{H}=\frac{c}{2\pi}\sqrt{S/(VL_{\mathrm{eff}})}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is the speed of sound, 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 the area of the hole, 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 the volume of the cells and 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{\mathrm{eff}}$
\end_inset

 the effective length of the hole, its real length plus an end correction of 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 times the equivalent radius. Against Elmer, the formula with the volume of the cells alone is too high by 0 to 10 per cent, and the inner correction seen by Elmer is 0.85 to 1.34 times the equivalent radius (mean 1.03), which gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 close to 1.9 for both ends together; the plans of 3 October 2026 ran with 1.55. The chain is the one wanted: Elmer calibrates the effective lengths on the real geometry, the small network runs the plan.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The plan sweeps the ratio of the chamber resonance to the frequency of the blade. Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:fenetre"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 gives, for a blade of 700 Hz in a chamber of 10.2 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset

 (the volume of chamber 12), the threshold pressure and the pitch at threshold. Below the resonance nothing starts. At a ratio of 1.1 the threshold is enormous and the note is pulled far down. Between 1.2 and 1.7 the threshold is lowest; above 2 it rises again. The study of 3 October 2026 concluded on a window of 1.2 to 1.7. The lower edge is itself uncertain: the 1000 Hz blade has its lowest threshold at 1.2 (423 Pa) and the 1400 Hz blade at 1.1 (775 Pa), where the plan stops being clean. It is the maker's window: a chamber whose resonance stands a fifth to a minor sixth above the tongue makes it speak most easily. The pitch at threshold tells the other side of the story: the closer the resonance, the more it pulls the note down, from 36 cents at 1.2 to 6 cents at 2.0. The same tendencies hold in the real R12 chambers 1 and 12 computed by Elmer, with their proper volumes.
\end_layout

