\begin_layout Subsection
Application : Bend technics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A bend is a note pulled away from its pitch by the player, with the hands or the bellows, and brought back. On a free reed the levers are few, and the models of this work name them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first lever is pressure. In every version of the models that starts, the note goes down when the player pushes harder: the one-reed model gives minus 15.7 cents from 1.3 to 2 times the threshold, and the two-reed network a few cents below the blade at threshold, falling further with pressure. This is the sense that every player knows. The numbers are predictions of models whose amplitude is not yet bounded correctly; the sign is robust.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second lever is the chamber, and here Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:fenetre"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 is the guide. A chamber tuned far above the tongue, as all the low chambers of the R12 are, hardly pulls the note: in the plan the pitch moves by one or two cents between 300 and 2000 Pa when the resonance is 1.3 times the note. A chamber tuned close to the tongue pulls hard: at a ratio of 1.02 the same blade plays 84 cents low at threshold and 97 cents low at 2000 Pa, but its threshold has risen from 208 to 1322 Pa. A bendable chamber is a hard-blowing chamber, in the model. The experiment with modelling clay (E4 of the audit, halving a chamber far from resonance) predicts a slightly harder start and almost no pitch change; it tests the same point from the other side.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The third lever is the air path above the tongue, hole and pallet. In the version of the two-reed network with the closing section, the mass of air of that path is part of the engine: dividing it by ten, the tongue does not start; multiplying it by three, it starts less well. A pallet half closed changes that mass. Whether it also changes the pitch by more than the 0.1 cent that the tuner of this work resolves is not known.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): on the bench, one tongue at fixed pressure, the pallet angle swept over its eight steps (the Clapet factor of the plan of experiments), the pitch read by the tuner in cents at each step. Then the same with the pressure staircase of the wind tunnel protocol, going up and down. Two curves, one afternoon.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Reed coupling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A tongue is never alone. It is coupled to its plate, the plate to the block, the block to the soundboard, and through the air to the other tongue of its note. This chapter takes these couplings one by one, from the pot of wax to the pair of voices that beat.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Wax or nails ? or ...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hot wax poured around the plate, or nails driven through it into the wood with a strip of leather beneath: the two ways of fixing a plate have their partisans. What does the fixing do to the tongue? Two things, in principle. It seals the plate against the block, and it sets the boundary condition of the plate, which is the ground on which the tongue is clamped.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the second point this work has one number, and it is about the tongue, not the plate. In Elmer, replacing an ideal clamping of the tongue by its real one, a heel pressed from below at the rivet, lowers the first frequency by 0.3 per cent, about 5 cents. The plate itself is rigid in every model of this work; a plate held only by wax at its edges may flex, and nothing here has computed or measured by how much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the first point, sealing, the figure is again the proposed workshop criterion: a block with its plates, bare or furnished, under 0.05 litre per minute at 500 Pa. A leak at the edge of a plate is a second slot in parallel with the tongue; the workshop knows its symptoms, the pressure that falls before the note and the soft attack, and sometimes a neighbouring note that sounds faintly without being played.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): one plate, plucked three times waxed and three times nailed, alternately, with the chain of chapter 2 (microphone at 15 cm, fixed gain, pince_modes.py). The frequency is measured to better than 0.3 cent and the damping to 3 per cent on synthetic sounds, but remounting a plate moves the frequency by more than the shift of the chamber itself, hence the alternation. Does the fixing change the damping of the first mode? Which plate?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Glued reed block or wise
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The title keeps a French word: vis, screws. A block glued on the soundboard against a block held by screws, with a gasket between. The glued block is tight by nature and cannot be taken off; the screwed block comes off for tuning and relies on its gasket. The physics of this work has nothing to compare the two yet, but it has the test that would.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The test is the one Ewen already does at the tuning table, written here so that it can be repeated the same way. The block is on the table at a fixed pressure, always the same for a given type of block, 500 Pa for example; the flow meter reads the air that passes. Ten readings in five seconds give the free flow and its scatter. Then a finger or a weight presses the joint, always at the same place with the same force, and after three seconds ten readings give the pressed flow. The difference is the leak of that joint, in litres per minute. Three pairs are taken, and the joint leaks if the difference exceeds three times the scatter at rest in all three. What the test does not see is a leak elsewhere, which the finger does not close; for the whole block the pressure decay test of the bench gives the global leak, and the appendix on leak detection gives the ways to locate one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the leak at 500 Pa of a glued block and of a screwed block of the same instrument, by the test above. And a question only you can answer: when a screwed block is taken off and put back, does any tongue change pitch by more than the few cents that the chamber itself shifts it?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Parallel or perpendicular design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Plates can lie flat on the soundboard, parallel to it, or stand on the walls of a block, perpendicular to it, which is the common design. What changes is the shape of the chamber behind each tongue and the road from the slot to the hole. The method of chapter 2 is made for exactly this comparison, since the input impedance seen from the slot is computed on whatever geometry is drawn; but only the perpendicular design, the R12 block, has been drawn and computed. A geometry of tongue and cavity drawn by Ewen in 2020 (the Elmer project Reed_with_cav, FreeCAD, four bodies) was never computed, no solver being active in it; it is kept, not as a duplicate but as the starting point of the other design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One result of chapter 2 bears on the choice without deciding it. What the tongue feels at its frequency is the inertia of the air escaping through the hole, not the stiffness of the cavity. A flat plate with a short road to its hole and a standing plate with a long cell behind it will give the tongue different masses of air, and Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:fenetre"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 says that the mass, through the resonance it sets, moves the threshold and the pitch. Which design is better is therefore a question of where its resonance falls, and that is computed in minutes once the drawing exists.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the dimensions of one flat-mounted chamber of an instrument you have, so that Elmer can give its impedance seen from the slot next to that of a standing chamber of the R12.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Associate reeds in parallel / series
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A note of an accordion in the musette tuning is two tongues, sometimes three, that take their air from the same bellows and give it back through the same pallet. They share the air, and they talk to each other through it. This is the parallel association, and it is the whole purpose of the two-reed network coupled_reeds: two tongues, two chambers, two holes, one channel under the pallet, one pallet. The channel common to the two is the coupling; an option puts both tongues in the same chamber for the strongest coupling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first thing the network taught is where the engine of each tongue must sit. In one version the start-up of the tongues was carried by a resistance shared at the pallet; the two voices then locked to the unison up to 80 cents of detuning, which no real musette does, since a musette beats. With the engine proper to each tongue, the voices detuned by 20 cents beat at 1.209 Hz, the difference of the blades (1.21 Hz). The common channel couples the voices, but weakly; the excitation of a tongue is its own.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What the network predicts, in its two successive versions, is that the voices pull each other near the unison. With the first mechanism (low A reference, 300 Pa, 4 s), the beat heard is 27 per cent slower than the difference of the blades at 1 cent of detuning, 20 per cent at 2 cents, 12 per cent at 3, and equal to it from 5 cents. With the mechanism of the closing section (A4, 1000 Pa), the two voices lock to one frequency up to about 2 cents of detuning (0.6 Hz), beat 12 per cent slow at 5 cents, and freely at 20 cents. Near the lock the beat is not the difference of the blades but 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{\Delta^{2}-\Delta_{L}^{2}}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 is the detuning and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{L}$
\end_inset

 the locking range, the law of Adler for two coupled oscillators; the network reproduces it without having been told. This is the physics behind a gesture of the tuner: two voices tuned close enough stick, and the musette dies. The tuner of this work (web/) separates the two tongues of a note in half a second and reads their beat; experiment E3 of the audit tunes one tongue with the other blocked, then frees it, and reads whether the beat survives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two further predictions, both conditioned on guessed dimensions: the air does not double with two tongues (1.56 times the flow of one in the first version, almost twice, minus 4 per cent, in the second), and two tongues in phase radiate better than one (efficiency 
\begin_inset Formula $2.9\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

 against 
\begin_inset Formula $1.5\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

, numbers not to be trusted in absolute value, see the section on radiation).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The series association, one tongue feeding the air to another through a shared cavity, has not been modelled as such. What comes nearest is the slot-stack model of the audit (slot_stack.py), built for the experiments of Ewen in which a flat tongue cut in a sheet of steel and held between two plates plays in both directions, and two harmonium plates mounted head to head play in both directions too. That model is honest about its failure: it reproduces the ordinary mounting and refuses to make the sandwich play, which means that the real mechanism contains something it does not, a delay of the flow at the edge of the tongue and a pressure that is not uniform along it. A series association of tongues will be understood when that mechanism is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): experiment E3 on one musette note of your instrument, with the tuner: tune one tongue with the other blocked by a strip of paper, free it, read the beat in beats per minute at 1, 2, 3 and 5 cents of detuning. Does the beat disappear below about 2 cents, as the model says? And the sandwich: its dimensions, the plucked note of the blade, the played note in each direction against pressure with the U-tube.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Dissipation in cavities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where does the energy go? A tongue plucked in the air rings for seconds: its own damping is small, a damping ratio of 0.004 is the guessed value of the models, to be measured by gesture (a) of chapter 2. Blown, the same tongue settles in a cycle of fixed amplitude in a second or two: something takes energy at each cycle as fast as the breath gives it. In the two-reed network that something is named: the orifice losses of the hole and of the pallet, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho q|q|/(2(C_{d}S)^{2})$
\end_inset

. It is there that the ordered motion of the air becomes heat, and it is this loss that bounds the amplitude in the network, without any resistance adjusted by hand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The linear theory explains a thing the maker sees without naming it. A chamber helps the tongue only if its impedance, at the frequency of play, is dominated by its compliance, so that the pressure lags the flow by a quarter of a period and the force on the tongue has a part in phase with its velocity. A real hole ventilates the chamber: its resonance lies above the whole keyboard, the impedance is inertial almost everywhere, and the chamber damps instead of helping. This is why the honest network, with real losses and the old law of opening, did not start at all: the growth rate stayed negative whatever was added, at the damping of the blade alone, minus 2.64 per second, or lower. It is also consistent with the measurement of Ricot, Caussé and Misdariis 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "ricot2005"
literal "true"

\end_inset
, who showed that an accordion reed starts even without any acoustic coupling, by the aerodynamics of the flow in its slot. The chamber is not the engine; the engine is in the slot, and the chamber mostly takes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the slot itself the losses are viscous, and that was checked with a flow computation. In a clearance of 30 micrometres between tongue and plate the Reynolds number is about 80: the flow is a Poiseuille flow, not a jet, and the law of Bernoulli alone is wrong there. On a two-dimensional section of the sandwich reed, Elmer found that 80 per cent of the pressure drop in that clearance is viscous, and its flow agreed to 0.4 per cent with the one-dimensional law, Poiseuille plus Bernoulli, of slot_stack.py. The air of the slot answers in about a microsecond, two thousand times faster than a period of the tongue, so the flow in the slot is taken as instantaneous; only the air the tongue drags along keeps its inertia, as an added mass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The finite element model of the chamber has no loss at all, and says so. The resistance of a real chamber comes from gesture (b): a resonator with a quality factor of 20, which is the order used in the synthetic tests, loses 40 dB in 25 ms, and the analysis finds its damping to 5 per cent. On the bench, a leak is seen as a drop of the quality factor of the air resonances of the closed instrument, measured by a sweep (frf.py) and a ring-down; it does not locate the leak but it quantifies it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the quality factor of one chamber of the R12, closed by its plate, excited by a sweep from a small loudspeaker and recorded at 15 cm, with and without the plate waxed. The loss resistance that the models lack is this one number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Entropy and least energy state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Question 3 of the introduction asks whether the reeds work in a state of least energy. The short answer of this work is no, and the long answer is more interesting than a yes would have been.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A tongue at rest in its slot, with no air moving, is in its state of least energy: the spring of the blade is slack, the air is still. A tongue that sings is not in such a state. It is a steady state kept alive by a flow of energy: the bellows gives work at a rate equal to the pressure times the flow, the orifices and the viscous clearance turn almost all of it into heat, and a tiny ordered part leaves as sound. The models of this work put that part between a few hundred-thousandths and a few per cent, and do not trust either figure. What is certain is the architecture: an oscillator kept away from rest by a source, bounded by dissipation. Such a state is called a limit cycle; it is a state of balance, not of minimum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The balance can be written. Linearised around the equilibrium of a tongue held open by the pressure, the flow through the slot acts back on the tongue with a delay set by the compliance of the chamber, and the force of pressure acquires a part in phase with the velocity. In the one-reed model that anti-damping is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Gamma\,a\,\frac{\partial q_{\mathrm{out}}/\partial y}{\left(a\,\partial q_{\mathrm{out}}/\partial p\right)^{2}+\omega^{2}},\qquad a=\frac{\gamma P_{\mathrm{atm}}}{V_{0}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

 is the area swept by the tongue, 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 the stiffness of the spring of air in a chamber of volume 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial q_{\mathrm{out}}/\partial y$
\end_inset

 the modulation of the flow by the position of the tongue, 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial q_{\mathrm{out}}/\partial p$
\end_inset

 its dependence on pressure, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 the angular frequency. The tongue starts when this term exceeds its own damping, 
\begin_inset Formula $2\zeta\omega m$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset

 the damping ratio and 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 the modal mass. The formula also says when it stops: if the opening saturates because the tongue is blown out of its slot, the modulation 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial q_{\mathrm{out}}/\partial y$
\end_inset

 vanishes and the anti-damping with it, whatever the pressure. That is the choking every player knows, and the model produced it without being asked. The oscillation is bounded on both sides, by too little energy and by too much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What the maker minimises is not an energy but a pressure: the pressure at which the tongue starts. Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:fenetre"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 showed that the threshold is lowest when the chamber resonance stands 1.2 to 1.7 times above the note. The maker who finds that window by ear is doing what the plan of experiments does by computation: making the state of self-sustained oscillation reachable with the least breath.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Entropy enters this work in two places, and both are modest. The first is the word adiabatic. The air of a chamber is compressed and relaxed hundreds of times a second, too fast to exchange heat with the walls; its entropy stays constant during a cycle, which is why the spring of air has the stiffness 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma P_{\mathrm{atm}}/V$
\end_inset

 and not 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{\mathrm{atm}}/V$
\end_inset

. The second is the orifices, where the entropy is produced: the kinetic energy of a jet that has passed a hole is not recovered on the other side, it becomes heat, and the term 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho q|q|/(2(C_{d}S)^{2})$
\end_inset

 is the account of that irreversibility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a third place where the idea of a least state returns, and it is the one this book cares most about. Between the pressure where the tongue starts, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset

, and the lower pressure where it stops, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{off}}$
\end_inset

, two states coexist: the silent tongue and the singing one. In the one-reed model the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}/p_{\mathrm{off}}$
\end_inset

 came out at 1.33 (25.8 Pa over 19.4 Pa, for a model version whose thresholds are known to be too low in the bass); the bench measures the same two pressures by a ramp up and a ramp down (seuil.py). Neither state is the least state; the one the tongue is in depends on where it comes from. The tools of the stochastic chapter of the research bench see this as a potential with two wells, reconstructed from the measured drift and diffusion of the signal, and the noise of the breath as what can push the system over the barrier from one well to the other. A reed does not seek its least energy. It stays where it is until something, pressure or noise, moves it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the hysteresis ratio of one real tongue, by the staircase of the wind tunnel protocol, up and then down, three cycles. If the measured ratio is far from 1.33, the effective volume and the source impedance are the first two parameters to recalibrate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Mechanics and valves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The air that does not sing is the subject of this part. An accordion is a box of pressure, and every joint of it is a place where the air may leave without a sound. The valves, the gaskets, the pallets and the keys that lift them decide how much of the breath reaches the tongues, and how fast.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Leaks, flow channels and valves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A valve, in the language of the workshop, is the flap of leather or plastic glued over the slot of a tongue on the side from which the air does not come. When the plate is blown from the other side, the flap lifts and the tongue plays; when the air comes from its side, the flap closes the slot of the tongue that must stay silent, so that the air of the bellows is not wasted through it. The valve is why a plate with two tongues gives one note on the push and one on the draw. In the models of this work it appears only as a hypothesis: the flow through the slot is one-way, the valve is perfect. The audit says what is ignored: a leather valve has a mass, it leaks, and it slaps. In the experimental protocol of chapter 2 the valve of the tested tongue is removed or lifted, because lying on the slot on the chamber side it would decouple the chamber; the protocol lists the valve as a factor in its own right, in no model yet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The figures of this chapter come from the training booklet of Ewen and from his workshop practice. They are proposed criteria, not yet measured on his instruments, and they are given with that status. A valve should leak less than 0.02 litre per minute under 500 Pa, and no valve should lift under 1.5 times the maximum playing pressure. The bellows alone should leak less than 1 litre per minute at 500 Pa, a glued block bare or furnished less than 0.05, and the closed instrument less than 0.5. Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:trou"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 translates a leak into a hole: at 500 Pa, a round hole of 1 mm passes 0.8 litre per minute. These flows follow the law of an orifice with a contraction coefficient close to 0.6, the value 0.61 used in the models for a sharp-edged jet; the check was made here, the figures are the booklet's.
\end_layout

