\begin_layout Standard
Two instruments cost nothing. A gasometer is a box turned upside down and floating in a bucket of water: filled with air, it feeds the instrument and sinks as the air leaks out, and for the box of the workshop a descent of 6.7 cm per minute is 1 litre per minute. A U-tube of transparent hose half filled with water and a ruler is a manometer: 1 mm of water is about 10 Pa (9.81 Pa exactly). With these two, the criteria above can be checked on any instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What a leak does to the music is known in the workshop and explained by the network. A leak in parallel with the tongues takes flow from a source of finite resistance, so the pressure falls before the note: the attack is soft, and a pianissimo starts badly. A leaking valve lets air through the slot of the silent tongue of its plate, and if that tongue is close to its threshold it sounds faintly: the ghost note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Acoustic transmittance of valves
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A closed valve, a closed pallet, a mute: each is a thin wall between a cavity full of sound and the outside. How much sound passes through it is its transmittance, usually written as a transmission loss in decibels. Nothing in this work has measured one yet; the method and the software are ready, and they are cheap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rigorous way is the transfer matrix method with four microphones (standard ASTM E2611): a tube, the sample in the middle, two microphones upstream and two downstream, and two different terminations so that the four coefficients of the matrix can be separated. The transmission loss follows from the matrix as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{TL}=20\log_{10}\left(|T_{11}+T_{12}/\rho c+\rho c\,T_{21}+T_{22}|/2\right)$
\end_inset

 for a tube of constant section and an anechoic end, where 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{11}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{22}$
\end_inset

 are the four coefficients and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho c$
\end_inset

 the characteristic impedance of air (banc_recherche/transfer.py, transfer_matrix_two_load and tl_from_matrix). The cheap way uses the two microphones of the impedance tube and compares the spectra upstream and downstream, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{TL}=10\log_{10}(S_{\mathrm{in}}/S_{\mathrm{out}})$
\end_inset

, a coarse estimate, honest about being one. The sound card has three channels; the four-microphone method would need a second pass with the microphones moved, or a second card.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One thing the models already say about the valve is not about transmittance but about pitch, and it deserves a place here because it is surprising. In the audit of the one-reed model, an opening symmetric in the position of the tongue, 
\begin_inset Formula $S=b|y|$
\end_inset

, modulated the slot twice per period and the model played 470 Hz for a tongue of 220 Hz: an octave wrong. The flow through a free reed is one-way, and in the reading of the models it is the valve that makes it so. Whether the real tongue would behave like that without its valve is a question for the sandwich experiments, where a tongue without any valve plays in both directions; the point stands that the valve is not an accessory of the plate but part of what fixes the note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the transmission loss of one leather valve and one plastic valve glued on a plate, in a tube with two microphones, by the coarse method first. A difference of a few decibels between the two materials would already be a result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Sealing material influence of sound radiated
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Leather or plastic for the valves, felt or foam for the gaskets, wax or leather under the plates: the sealing materials are chosen by the hand and judged by the ear. Their influence on the radiated sound is of two kinds, and the second is the one usually forgotten.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first kind is what the material lets through when it is meant to seal. A leak is a place where air escapes without making a note, and it is not silent: a jet through a small orifice under pressure becomes turbulent and radiates a broadband hiss that rises high in frequency, into the ultrasound. In a recording, the hiss of a leak is rich where the voice of the tongue and the noise of the room are poor, above a few kilohertz. This is the handle by which the appendix on leak detection catches it: filter high, take the envelope of the hiss, and, if the pressure of the bellows is modulated at a known slow frequency, keep only what blinks at that frequency. The bellows of the bench, which already makes controlled strokes at 2 to 10 Hz, is the modulator; the synchronous detection is a few lines of code (banc_recherche/leak.py, lockin and leak_map); a microphone walked over the surface gives a map. None of this has yet been run on a real instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second kind is what the material does when it moves. A valve lifts and falls at every change of direction of the bellows; it has a mass and it slaps. The models ignore it, and the tuner of this work detects the reversal of the bellows by a sharp and isolated dip of the signal without silence, which is where the slap lives. A stiff plastic valve and a supple leather one will not fall the same way, and the attack of the note after the reversal carries the difference. Nothing here has measured it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the same note recorded with its leather valve, then with a plastic one, at the same bellows pressure, with the tuner exporting the WAV; compare the spectra above 4 kHz during the held note (the hiss) and the first 50 ms after a reversal (the slap). Which note, and which instrument?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Dynamics of keyboard
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A key lifts a pallet. The pallet is the last door of the air road, and in the network it is an orifice: a mass of air 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\ell/S$
\end_inset

 and a loss, with guessed dimensions of 3 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

 of area, 10 mm of effective length and a lift of about 4 mm. On the bench it is an axis in degrees, swept from 2 to 22 degrees in the 2020 plan with a lever of 20 mm, or replaced by a fixed pallet and a timed electrovalve when only the arrival of the pressure matters. The keyboard of the bench is a matrix of electromagnets on the buttons, 24 on the left hand and 47 on the right, held with a reduced current once pressed; the firmware that drives it is written for an ESP32-S3 and has not yet been tested on hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What the speed of the key does to the note is a question of pressure rise. In the simulations the pressure of the bellows rises linearly in 20 ms, a hypothesis with no measurement behind it; a slow key gives the tongue a slow rise and a soft attack, a fast key a step. The response time of the note, from the opening to the steady sound, is already measured by the analysis chain of the research bench (the quantity called Tresp, computed with Praat 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "boersma_praat"
literal "true"

\end_inset
 through Parselmouth 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "jadoul2018parselmouth"
literal "true"

\end_inset
); what is missing is the position of the key against time next to it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a subtler point, and it is a prediction. In the version of the two-reed network with the closing section, the mass of air of the road above the tongue, hole and pallet together, is part of the mechanism of start-up: with that mass divided by ten the tongue does not start, with it multiplied by three it starts less well, and there is an optimum. A pallet barely open is a long thin passage and a large mass of air; a pallet wide open a small one. If the prediction holds, the depth of the key changes the ease of the start, not only the loudness. Experiment E2 of the audit is designed to see it: the starting pressure, read on the U-tube, against the lift of the pallet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the force and travel of one key, with a load cell (HX711) and a small magnetic encoder (AS5600) on the bench, a new channel of the firmware proposed in the measurement map but not built. And experiment E2: the starting pressure of one tongue for five lifts of its pallet, set with shims. Is there an optimum, as the model says?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Pedale avec enclenchement manuel de notes type stylo bille
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The title describes a device in French: a pedal, or a latch worked by the hand, that holds a note open the way the button of a ballpoint pen holds its tip out, one press to engage and one to release. The musical purpose is a drone, a note that goes on sounding while the fingers are free. The design is Ewen's and is not in the repository; what is here is what the physics of the previous chapters says about such a note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A latched pallet is a constant opening. The tongue behind it then depends on one thing only, the pressure of the bellows, and that pressure is shared with every other note being played. The audit of the one-reed model takes as its working hypothesis that the bellows is a source of flow with a finite internal resistance, not a source of pressure, and gives the proof in the playing: at a constant speed of the bellows, opening a chord makes the pressure fall, where a source of pressure would hold it and double the flow. A drone therefore costs pressure for the melody, and the cost is the flow of the drone tongue times the resistance of the source. The flows that the models give per tongue (0.1 to 0.4 litre per second at 1000 Pa) are known to be too large; the real one is read on the flow meter of the bench in a minute.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second thing the physics says is about the two directions. A latched note on a plate with two tongues sounds one tongue on the push and the other on the draw, and the two tongues of a plate are two different blades: on Ewen's own instrument the tuner found differences of 15 to 25 cents between push and draw on the same notes. A drone that must stay in tune through the reversal of the bellows asks for the two tongues of its plate to be tuned together more carefully than the melody does.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the mechanism itself, and its question for the bench: how much flow the drone tongue takes at playing pressure, and by how much the pressure available to the other notes falls when it is latched, measured with the flow meter and the pressure sensor, or the U-tube.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Bend en bout de course du clavier
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second French title names a key with extra travel: pressed to the bottom of its ordinary stroke the key opens the pallet, and pressed further it does something more, which bends the note. What can that something be? The models of this work name three levers, in the subsection on bend techniques above, and here they are weighed for a key.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The key cannot change the pressure of the bellows; that lever belongs to the arm. It can change the opening of the pallet: beyond the ordinary stroke it may close the pallet partly again, or open a second, smaller passage. In the two-reed network this changes the mass of air of the road above the tongue, which the model places in the mechanism of start-up, and the pitch effect of it is not known: it may be below what the ear notices. The key can also change the chamber: a second pallet at the end of the stroke could open a cell beside the chamber, changing its volume and its resonance. Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:fenetre"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 says that this lever is strong only when the chamber resonance is close to the note, within a ratio of 1.1 or so, and that such a chamber is hard to blow: 1322 Pa of threshold against 208 Pa at a ratio of 1.3, for the same blade in the plan. A bend at the end of the stroke is therefore, in the model, a trade between the depth of the bend and the ease of the note, and only a measurement will say where the trade is worth it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the mechanism you have in mind, and one measurement before building it: with modelling clay, reduce the volume of one chamber in two or three steps and read the pitch of its tongue with the tuner at a fixed pressure of the staircase. If the pitch moves by less than a few cents, the chamber is not the lever for that note, and the pallet is the one to try.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Reedblock to {Soundboard+box} coupling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The block does not float. It stands on the soundboard, and the soundboard is a wall of the box. Whatever the tongue does to the air, the air does to these pieces of wood, and they answer. This part has one chapter, and it is mostly a list of what has not been measured yet, with the one thing that has.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Soundboard
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The soundboard is the plate on which the block stands and in which the holes of the chambers are drilled; the pallets lie on its other face. In every model of this work it is rigid, and it enters only by its holes. That is already a great deal: the hole is the part of the chamber that the tongue feels most. Its effective length, computed by Elmer from the impedance seen from the slot, is 25.8 mm for chamber 1, 22.7 mm for chamber 6 and 30.4 mm for chamber 12, against a real thickness of 8 mm in the drawing. Two thirds of what the tongue feels as the mass of the hole is the air at its two ends, and the pallet, when it is close to the hole, lengthens it further; the finite element model takes the pallet far away, and this is a parameter to sweep.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A small sweep on chamber 1 shows how much the hole weighs. Keeping the cells and changing only the hole, the first resonance goes from 665 Hz (hole 4 by 12.5 mm, soundboard 8 mm, large outer correction) to 1314 Hz (hole 8 by 25 mm, soundboard 4 mm, small correction). A factor of four in area and a factor of two in thickness move the resonance by an octave; the volume of the chamber, which is what the maker usually thinks of first, was not touched.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The soundboard as a vibrating plate is absent from this work, and so is the box. The hypothesis of the network that the pallet is the only radiator is incomplete, as the audit says: the bellows, the box and the plate radiate too. How much, and at which frequencies, is a measurement that costs an impact hammer (a small screwdriver handle will do), a piezoelectric accelerometer of a few euros glued on the soundboard, and the third channel of the sound card: the frequency response from the tap to the acceleration gives the modes of the plate, their frequencies and their damping, with the same estimators the research bench already uses for tongues (the inter-spectrum of excitation.py, the ring-down and the matrix pencil 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
before ""
key "hua1990matrixpencil"
literal "true"

\end_inset
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the frequency response of the soundboard of one instrument, block mounted and block removed, by hammer and accelerometer. The question to answer is simple: does any mode of the plate fall within a few per cent of a note of the instrument, or of the resonance of a chamber? If none does, the rigid soundboard of the models is a fair assumption for that instrument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Other parts involved
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bellows that breathes, the grille that the sound crosses, the box that holds it all, the pallets and the mutes: the rest of the instrument. Each one appears in this work as an assumption of the models, and this part says which assumption, and what would replace it by a measurement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Bellows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bellows is the lung. Everything upstream of the tongue is in it: the pressure, its rise, its fall, its reversal. The models of this work treat it in the simplest ways, and the bench replaces it by a motor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Framed bellow
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A framed bellows is folds of cardboard and cloth, leather at the corners, and a wooden frame at each end that takes the block and the box. The workshop knows where it leaks: at the corners and at the frame. The proposed criterion of the booklet is less than 1 litre per minute at 500 Pa for the bellows alone, closed on a board, which by Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:trou"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
 is the equivalent of a round hole a little over 1 mm across.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is the bellows to the tongue? In the two-reed network it is an ideal source of pressure rising in 20 ms, a hypothesis without a measurement. In the one-reed model it is a source of flow with an internal resistance, 
\begin_inset Formula $q=q_{0}-p/R$
\end_inset

, and the audit argues from the playing that this is the right picture: a real bellows, like a real turbine, gives less flow when the pressure rises. The value of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 decides the amplitude of the note (section on the aperture) and moves the threshold: in one version of the one-reed model the starting pressure of the reference tongue was 23.0 Pa with an ideal source, 25.8 Pa with 
\begin_inset Formula $R=2\times10^{8}$
\end_inset

 and 35.9 Pa with 
\begin_inset Formula $5\times10^{7}$
\end_inset

 Pa s m
\begin_inset Formula $^{-3}$
\end_inset

, a softer source helping the start less, while the choking pressure stayed at 379.8 Pa. These are numbers of a model version whose bass thresholds are known to be far too low; the direction of the effect is what to keep.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The playing pressures of an accordion are of the order of 500 to 3000 Pa, in both signs; the thresholds of the tongues are far below, a few tens to a few hundreds of pascals in the models, where a real instrument asks for roughly a hundred to a thousand all over the keyboard. The bellows also has a compliance of its own and the hand behind it; the audit says both are measurable with a manometer and neither is in a model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the bench the bellows is motorised: a stepper axis on a linear table, the only source of air, driven at a set speed or servoed to a pressure, reversing at the ends of its finite stroke so that a measurement is made within one pass, push and draw being the two signs of pressure. The bench does not work at present and has never been rebuilt; the firmware exists and is untested; the flow meter is still there. The same motorised bellows is the modulator of the leak imaging method of the appendix, at 2 to 10 Hz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): two measurements on one of your bellows, closed on a board with a pressure tap. The leak at 500 Pa, by the gasometer or the flow meter, against the 1 litre per minute of the booklet. And the characteristic of your own arm as a source: pressure against flow at a steady push, through a known orifice, read on the U-tube and the gasometer; its slope is the 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 of the models. Which bellows, which day?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Timbral elements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the tongue has made the sound, several pieces of the instrument shape it before it reaches the ear. This chapter lists them, says what the physics of this work would predict for each, and admits that none has been measured here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Grid
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The grille is the perforated screen in front of the pallets of the right hand. The sound of every tongue crosses it. Physically it is a plate with many holes and a thin layer of air behind it: at low frequency it is transparent, since the wavelength is long compared with the holes and the depth; towards high frequencies the holes act as masses of air and the layer behind as a spring, and the grille becomes a filter whose cut-off depends on the fraction of open area and on the depth. Nothing in this work has computed or measured a grille, and no number is given here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): one note recorded with the grille on and off, same microphone position, same bellows pressure, with the WAV export of the tuner; compare the two spectra above 2 kHz. The difference, if any, is the grille.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Resonance box
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some instruments put a set of tongues behind a second chamber inside the box, a resonance box, which gives them their rounder sound. Physically it is one more cavity on the road of the air, and the method of chapter 2 applies to it unchanged: mesh its air, compute its modes and the impedance seen from the hole of the block. One caution: a resonance box is many centimetres long, and at one or two kilohertz that is no longer small compared with the wavelength; the lumped network of masses and springs stops being valid there, while the finite element model does not care, having been checked on a duct of 100 mm against the exact formula to a relative error of 
\begin_inset Formula $1.3\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

. No resonance box has been drawn or computed in this work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the dimensions of the resonance box of one instrument you can open, and the recording of one tongue through it and outside it, so that the computed modes can be set against the measured spectrum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Clapets et sourdines
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pallets and mutes, in French in the title. The pallet is the door of each note, and it has had its say in this book several times: an orifice of guessed dimensions in the network, the only radiator of the model, the mass of air that the model puts in the mechanism of start-up, the last place where the key acts. The register slides, which close whole sets of tongues, are of the same family, and the workshop counts them among the places that leak. A mute is anything, felt, wood or cloth, placed on the road of the sound to soften it: a longer and narrower passage, hence more mass of air and more loss, hence less high frequency and a little less of everything.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What is said of valves in the chapter on leaks applies to both: their transmittance can be measured in a tube with two or four microphones, the software is ready, the rig is not. What the models predict for the pallet, an optimum of its lift for the ease of the start, is experiment E2 and is unmeasured.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the transmission loss of one register slide, closed, in the two-microphone tube, by the coarse method of transfer.py. And the lift of your pallets at full key, with a feeler gauge: the 4 mm of the model is a guess.
\end_layout

